WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.416 --> 00:00:03.860
- So I put myself in a position

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.300
where I could get a record deal.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.930
Where I could find somebody.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.100
Where I could call somebody.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:08.933
Where I could say,

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:09.766
"Can I borrow you for a five minutes?"

00:00:09.766 --> 00:00:10.599
- Lemme interrupt, I apologize.

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:11.601
- That's the name of the show.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:13.071
- Thank you very much!

00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:15.904
(hard rock music)

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.820
- Everybody, it's Gary again.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.130
Back with another episode of my new show

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.600
that I'm super excited about called

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.090
Interrupted by Gary Vee.

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:35.980
Today's guest is Jesse
Itzler, an entrepreneur,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.630
but I'm gonna let him frame it up for you

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.500
and then I'm gonna fire away
with a bunch of questions.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.400
But first and foremost,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.700
Jess, thanks for being on.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.720
- Thanks for having me,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.553
I quite appreciate it.

00:00:44.553 --> 00:00:47.210
- So, tell the VaynerNation
that's listening right now

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.043
who you are.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.540
- Great, well, I'm a serial entrepreneur.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.270
I started out in the music business.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.490
I was signed to a record label called

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:57.310
Delicious Vinyl in the 90's--

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.340
- What year did you sign?

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.630
- I signed in 1990, I was 21.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.490
- And what kind of artist were you?

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.360
- Don't laugh.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:04.534
I was a rapper.

00:01:04.534 --> 00:01:06.190
- I'm not laughing!
(audience laughs)

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.700
- And I was a white
rapper, believe it or not!

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.680
- I'm aware, I can see it.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.530
For everybody who's listening,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.800
you'll hear some laughs in the background,

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.750
and things of that nature,
we're here in an off-site

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:15.680
kind of conference thing,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.160
so we're just recording the podcast

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.620
in between sessions, so

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.453
keep going, Jess.

00:01:20.453 --> 00:01:22.050
So you were a rapper at 21?

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.125
- Started out right--

00:01:23.125 --> 00:01:23.958
- Where'd you grow up?

00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:25.400
- I grew up in Long Island.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.830
And then I went to a
school in Washington, D.C.,

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.260
at American University.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.060
Got a record deal--
- Smart kid.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.490
- Good test-taker, maybe, I
don't know about smart, but.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:35.323
- Fair enough.

00:01:35.323 --> 00:01:36.730
- Got a record deal right out of college,

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:38.223
after a lot of rejection.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.480
Signed to Delicious Vinyl,

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.800
got dropped from the label a year later.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.060
- Why?

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.170
- Sales and just wasn't the
right thing and tough timing,

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:48.580
I was behind Vanilla Ice

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.293
and I wasn't nearly as good as Eminem.

00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:53.250
(laughs) Here I am.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:54.810
But it was the best thing
that ever happened to me.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.370
The worst thing at the time,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.680
'cause I only had two things
on my resume after that.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.650
I was a kiddie pool
attendant and I was a rapper.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.099
And that's not gonna
get you a lot of jobs.

00:02:03.099 --> 00:02:04.360
- How did the rapper thing go down?

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:05.860
How old are you?

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.270
- I'm gonna be 52.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:09.103
- Hey, you look great.

00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:11.110
So 52, so you're eight
years older than me.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.310
So when does rap hit your radar?

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:16.170
- Rap hits my radar right away, man.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.510
Right when it came on the scene.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.510
I was probably, I don't know,
16, 15, so early eighties.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.867
I heard "It's Like That" by Run-DMC,

00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:27.660
"Sucker MCs" by Run-DMC,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.730
fell in love with it.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.470
Grew up in Long Island where
hip hop was emerging fast,

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.670
and just got the bug.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:35.410
So started out breakdancing,

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.820
started out, you know, just
doing whatever I could do.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:38.990
This a different era, you know.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.050
Mid-eighties.
- I remember it.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:42.110
- And just got into it.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.500
And when everybody in
college was making resumes

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.880
and sending resumes to companies,

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.760
I'm like, "I'm getting a record deal!

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.100
I don't need a resume!"

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.790
And at that time I'd never
been in a studio, I--

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.320
- Were you an entrepreneurial kid?

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.870
- I was an insane kid.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:56.770
I tried everything.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.850
My parents gave me a long leash.

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.270
And I just tried anything
and they allowed me

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.460
and supported me to do anything like that.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.930
But I wouldn't say I was
a sharp entrepreneur,

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.050
I didn't have a paper route,
- Right.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:10.872
- No, but was just, I was exploring--

00:03:10.872 --> 00:03:12.730
- You were not looking to make money?

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.500
- I was looking for adventure, man.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.890
I was looking for fun, and I still am.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.610
But I was never really
wired to make money.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:20.443
- So what did your crew look like?

00:03:20.443 --> 00:03:22.550
Were you like a skateboard kid too?

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:23.975
- I was athletic.

00:03:23.975 --> 00:03:24.890
- Okay.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.530
- I was artistic, I
was a good storyteller.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.200
And I wasn't scared.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.799
I wasn't scared to fail.
- Why?

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:33.400
- I wasn't scared to try.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.850
'Cause my parents really
just supported my effort,

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.550
they praised my effort
and not the results.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.383
And--

00:03:39.383 --> 00:03:41.030
- Did they acknowledge when you failed?

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.600
- They praised the effort.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.800
So if we got blown out
of a basketball game,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.330
or if I had a great game or a bad game,

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.060
it was like, "You tried so hard."

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.730
And they've always praised the effort.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.160
And as a father of four,

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.152
that's something that we do with our kids,

00:03:54.152 --> 00:03:54.985
it's all about the effort.

00:03:54.985 --> 00:03:58.250
- Where do you the Mendoza line is on

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:01.910
praising effort and making
one appreciate process,

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:06.910
versus creating delusion when
one is not good at that thing?

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.920
- Well, I think that the fine line

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.130
in between those two
points is disappointment,

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.620
and I think disappointment's
been stripped from kids.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:14.453
- I agree.

00:04:14.453 --> 00:04:16.030
- There's participation
trophies, and you get,

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:17.560
you know, everyone's on,
you know, you can make the--

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:18.840
- So your parents on the way home

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:21.310
of losing a game 42 to 16 were like,

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:22.717
hey, I really appreciate the fact that

00:04:22.717 --> 00:04:25.390
you were down by 30,
that you were going hard,

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.690
but in the same token,
they weren't making excuses

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:30.050
or saying you're gonna be
Michael fuckin' Jordan.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.570
- Yeah, but my dad owned a
plumbing supply house, man.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.537
My dad didn't even get that deep into it.

00:04:34.537 --> 00:04:36.750
It was like, he took me to the game,

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:37.780
he watched it, and then he went

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.177
and he sold plumbing supplies, like--

00:04:39.177 --> 00:04:40.510
- Did he talk?

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:41.343
- Of course he talked!

00:04:41.343 --> 00:04:44.430
But it wasn't about raising this kid

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.070
that's gonna go to Harvard or that's gonna

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.440
be an amazing athlete,
it was like letting me--

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.130
- So, you went hard,

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:50.430
and you get in the car,

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.280
and he says, "Good job, kid?"

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.346
- He says, "How'd you feel about it?"

00:04:55.346 --> 00:04:56.179
You know?

00:04:56.179 --> 00:04:57.012
- That's a really good one.

00:04:57.012 --> 00:04:57.845
- Yeah.

00:04:57.845 --> 00:04:58.678
- I like that.

00:04:58.678 --> 00:05:00.420
- And then I say, "I feel
terrible, we got blown out."

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.253
- Right.

00:05:01.253 --> 00:05:02.086
- And then he'd say like,

00:05:02.086 --> 00:05:03.330
"Well you know, you did
great, and keep trying,"

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.430
and that kind of stuff.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:06.730
My father wasn't the kind of person to

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:09.640
put any kind of pressure on me to succeed.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.110
He was a family guy, he still is,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.020
and it was just about that.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:16.060
- And mom?

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.930
- Teacher and then raised four kids.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:20.027
- And what was she saying to you?

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.394
- Same thing, it was just--

00:05:23.394 --> 00:05:24.840
- Did she tell you, "You
look so cute in your shorts?"

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.390
Like was she that kind of mom?

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:27.347
My mom was!

00:05:27.347 --> 00:05:28.180
She was like, "You looked great too."

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.440
I was like, "Thanks, Mom!"

00:05:30.423 --> 00:05:32.500
I think I was 16 when I first realized,

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.061
wait a minute, I'm not the
best-looking person on Earth?

00:05:35.061 --> 00:05:36.990
Like, you know?

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.604
Was she that kind of mom?

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:42.160
- She was, they really
gave me my distance.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.280
And they let me figure it out--

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.113
- What number were you in the kids?

00:05:44.113 --> 00:05:45.539
- I was the bottom, four.

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:46.660
- (laughs) Got it.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:47.681
It's cool though!

00:05:47.681 --> 00:05:48.514
- It's very cool.
- It is cool!

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.830
Like they've been
through enough reps where

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.930
I'm sure you're, is it a sister

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:54.200
or brother that's the oldest?

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.033
- Brother.

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:56.200
- Had it, slightly different.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.200
- Yeah.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.033
Yeah.

00:05:58.033 --> 00:05:59.770
It was the best kind of
childhood for me though, man.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.640
There was no, I didn't feel
any pressure to have to,

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:02.820
it was, you know what they did?

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.240
They put the pressure on me.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:05.211
They made me--

00:06:05.211 --> 00:06:06.044
- By not putting the pressure--

00:06:06.044 --> 00:06:07.680
- They made me want it for me.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.100
It can't be my parents' dream,

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.321
it would have had to be my dream.

00:06:10.321 --> 00:06:11.154
- I get it.

00:06:11.154 --> 00:06:12.910
- And they never, I remember
when I wanted to get

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.300
good grades for my
parents, my grades sucked.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.640
When I was, I want to get good grades,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.470
so I could get to a good school
for me, they skyrocketed.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:24.060
So they took the pressure
off of me wanting--

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.070
- The freedom created the results.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:26.500
- It was my goal!

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:27.730
It was my dream!

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:29.760
- So now, you get signed,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.510
tell them about the New York Knicks song.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.150
- I get signed to this label
called Delicious Vinyl,

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.690
I wrote a couple songs for
an artist named Tone Loc,

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.700
I get dropped from the label a year later,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:39.990
I move back to New York City,

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.240
I have two things on my resume,

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.120
I was a kiddie pool
attendant, and I was a rapper.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:45.220
And, but

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:48.440
my wife always says, this is so true,

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.650
you find your passion
at the intersection of

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.060
what it is you love to do
and what you're good at,

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.600
and providing a product
that people like or enjoy,

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.210
I love sports and music,

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:00.550
so I married those two things
and I wrote a theme song

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.560
for the New York Knicks, it
was called "Go, New York, Go,"

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.210
I was 22,

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.620
and the Knicks paid me
$4,000 for the song,

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.150
it cost me $4,800 to do the song,

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:11.600
you asked me if I was into money.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.640
Not really, I lost $800
on my first big trade.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.970
But the reality is, I
would have paid the Knicks

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.200
10 grand to do this song,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:21.210
and the song became the number one

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:23.610
most requested song on New York radio, and

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:24.580
that took off--

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.270
- As a 44 year old Knicks fan,

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:28.570
you can't imagine what
that song actually is.

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:31.970
It was being played while
the Knicks made a run

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.010
and actually got to the NBA finals,

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.650
lost unfortunately in seven games

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.990
to the Rockets, but

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.360
I probably heard that song

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.620
8,000 times, 7,800 of them

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.068
were in a four-week period in 1994.

00:07:47.068 --> 00:07:48.050
(laughter)

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:48.883
- Yes.

00:07:48.883 --> 00:07:49.890
- It was insane.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.348
- It was insane, and--

00:07:51.348 --> 00:07:54.680
- That became your, tell
me living that moment,

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:58.330
where June, 1994, you're
eight years older than me,

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.310
so you're 22, right?

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:01.850
22, 23,

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.270
are you basically, you know,

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.650
the internet's not around,
so people don't know shit

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:08.690
the same way, are you
just walking around life,

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:10.590
and before you even say
hi, you telling people

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:11.860
that you wrote that fuckin' song?

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:12.693
I would be.

00:08:12.693 --> 00:08:13.686
- Yeah, well.

00:08:13.686 --> 00:08:14.519
(laughter)

00:08:14.519 --> 00:08:16.070
It was interesting, 'cause
at that point of my life,

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:18.090
I slept on 18 different couches.

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:18.923
- [Gary] Yep.

00:08:18.923 --> 00:08:20.930
- So I had 18 different
friends, I bounced around

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.340
for a week here at a
couch, couch, couch, couch,

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.260
and then all of a sudden,
I wrote this song,

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.192
and it's the number one
song, you know, at the time,

00:08:28.192 --> 00:08:29.550
during the, it was big.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:30.383
And--

00:08:30.383 --> 00:08:31.216
- [Gary] Big big.

00:08:31.216 --> 00:08:33.470
In New York, uncomfortably big.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:34.303
- Yeah.

00:08:34.303 --> 00:08:37.320
And I didn't really know how
to handle that, you know?

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.426
But everyone was asking
me for playoff tickets.

00:08:40.426 --> 00:08:41.630
(laughter)

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:43.780
And I had no money, I
was sleeping on couches,

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:44.860
and you know what I did?

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:46.500
I got everybody playoff tickets.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.333
- [Gary] That's cool.

00:08:47.333 --> 00:08:48.770
- Yeah, on my credit card.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:52.550
And I realized at an early
age that to be a connector,

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.750
and not having to ask for
anything in return and just,

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.790
I knew it would come back ten-fold,

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.500
and I wanted to be the guy
that could get anything done,

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:00.333
you know?

00:09:00.333 --> 00:09:01.880
- [Gary] That was what
you wanted to stand for?

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:02.940
- I still do.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.703
Anything, there's no
obstacle, there's no business,

00:09:04.703 --> 00:09:07.110
there's no goal, there's no race.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:08.530
- [Gary] So how much in playoff tickets

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:11.400
did you end up spending?

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.360
- Enough that it set me back.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:14.193
- [Gary] Yeah!

00:09:14.193 --> 00:09:17.790
- But again, those seats,
those seeds, you know

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.532
were planted, yeah, they paid off later.

00:09:19.532 --> 00:09:20.960
- [Gary] So what happened next?

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:23.480
- So all of a sudden I was like, whoa,

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:24.960
as an entrepreneur, you guys,

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.840
we have a lot of
entrepreneurs in this room.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.390
You realize that you look for something

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:31.180
to differentiate yourself.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.330
And this was a big differentiator for me,

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.850
I was like, no one else
is doing sports music,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.290
everyone is trying to be
Vanilla Ice at the time,

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.260
or whatever, but no
one's writing theme songs

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:41.910
for pro sports teams.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.820
So that was my a ha moment,

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.680
and I started, I set up a
company to write theme songs

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:47.570
for other teams.

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:48.403
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:09:48.403 --> 00:09:51.730
- And I'm like, I ended up
doing almost every team,

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:52.956
and then I sold that company to--

00:09:52.956 --> 00:09:53.789
- In the NBA?

00:09:53.789 --> 00:09:56.260
- NBA, and some baseball and football.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.110
- What was the most next famous song?

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:02.200
- Well, what happened was, the
songs became somewhat popular

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.821
in each region, but I
turned it into selling them

00:10:04.821 --> 00:10:06.986
as a compilation with all the songs that--

00:10:06.986 --> 00:10:08.700
- That I understand,
but in its own region--

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:10.730
- Chicago, 'cause Jordan came back,

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.630
there was a song called
"Right Back In It."

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:14.430
But I sold almost a million units.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:17.010
So it became a business,
and I sold that company

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:18.290
to a public company.

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:19.880
- And in that transaction,

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:21.070
did you make some money?

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:21.903
- Yes.

00:10:21.903 --> 00:10:24.210
- Did you have to work
there for a little bit?

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.600
- I worked, we had an earn out,

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.610
so I worked there for a year and a half,

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.190
and then we hit the earn out, and--

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.530
- Talk to me about that year and a half.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.130
- Well, in our contract, they paid us

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:34.460
a couple million dollars up front,

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.950
but we had a 16 million
dollar trigger if we hit

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.990
X amount in profit, so we
literally had a calendar,

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:41.460
we had three years to do it.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:42.810
And I'm like, every day I was like,

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:45.920
you've got 364 more days, man!

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:47.480
We gotta hit this number!

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.860
Every day was a countdown, and

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.500
that's how I, I mean, and we hit it.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:54.410
- So you're wildly
motivated, interest aligned?

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:55.880
- Yeah.

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.190
- You hit it, you're out.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:58.110
What happens next?

00:10:58.110 --> 00:10:59.500
Now you have some money.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.340
- What happened next was--

00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:01.173
- How old are you?

00:11:01.173 --> 00:11:03.127
- I'm 20, at the time, maybe 26.

00:11:03.127 --> 00:11:04.280
- And you have some money.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:05.113
- Mm-hmm.

00:11:05.113 --> 00:11:06.950
- Like way more than you ever had before.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:07.783
- Yeah.

00:11:07.783 --> 00:11:08.840
Anyone in my family.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:11.890
- And, do you go crazy, do you
do dumb shit, what do you do?

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:12.889
- No, I mean,

00:11:12.889 --> 00:11:15.657
my life was pretty simple,
it was pretty simple then,

00:11:15.657 --> 00:11:17.170
and pretty simple now.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:20.160
But I was still 26, 27 years old,

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.510
but what happened was, the
guy that bought our company

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:24.730
had a timeshare on a private jet.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:26.690
And he invited me at 27 years old

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:28.070
on to his private plane, it was like

00:11:28.070 --> 00:11:29.735
when I walked on it was like,

00:11:29.735 --> 00:11:30.720
I don't know if you guys
saw "The Wizard of Oz,"

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.220
it was like the scene
in "The Wizard of Oz"

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:34.570
when everything goes from
black and white to color,

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:35.403
it's like, what?

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:37.363
People fly like this?

00:11:37.363 --> 00:11:38.580
I was like, you gotta be kidding me!

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:39.830
I want to fly like this!

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:41.640
So by the time we landed,
my partner and I were like,

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.120
let's start a private jet company.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:45.390
So we can fly on private
planes when we travel.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:46.410
I was like, definitely!

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.160
Except we had no airplanes.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.946
But we had an idea, and
we took that idea to--

00:11:50.946 --> 00:11:52.190
- And who was that partner?

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:53.710
- His name is Kenny Dichter.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.078
And we took that idea to--

00:11:55.078 --> 00:11:56.550
- And how did you meet Kenny?

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:00.050
- Kenny, so I owned the trademark
to all these theme songs,

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:00.883
the songs that I was writing

00:12:00.883 --> 00:12:02.650
for the sports teams were popular,

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:04.760
but the trademarks were more valuable.

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.300
So "Go, New York, Go" was
being used by Budweiser,

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.020
Foot Locker, all these big companies,

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:10.200
and I wasn't getting any money,

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.490
'cause I sold the song for four grand.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:15.670
So I got smarter, and I
started licensing the song

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:16.840
and keeping the marks.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:18.230
Kenny owned a clothing company,

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:20.800
so I'm like, we make "Go, New
York, Go" tee shirts and hats,

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:22.390
and we started working together.

00:12:22.390 --> 00:12:25.590
And, you know, built a
big business out of it.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:26.987
Then I was a guest, I'm like,

00:12:26.987 --> 00:12:28.352
"Let's start a private jet company,"

00:12:28.352 --> 00:12:30.350
he was like, "Definitely."

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.800
And we took a meeting with
a company called NetJets,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.430
owned by Warren Buffett, the
largest private jet company

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:37.263
in the world--

00:12:37.263 --> 00:12:39.060
- How did you get to that meeting?

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.000
- Crazy story, man.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.220
The president of NetJets's
daughter had a sweet 16,

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.140
and Christina Aguilera was performing.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:48.527
And through somebody that knew me,

00:12:48.527 --> 00:12:50.540
and they knew that I knew the manager,

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:51.373
and called up and like,

00:12:51.373 --> 00:12:53.710
"This guy wants something
special for his daughter."

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:56.190
And like I said, nothing's impossible.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.100
I called up, and the
next day at the concert,

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:00.800
she was a background
singer with the mic off.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:01.677
And he called me up, he was like,

00:13:01.677 --> 00:13:03.370
"I don't know who the fuck you are,

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:06.540
or what you do, but my
daughter is a hero in our town.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:08.850
If you ever need anything, let me know."

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:12.240
A year later, I need 650 airplanes.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:13.178
Jim?

00:13:13.178 --> 00:13:14.782
(laughter)

00:13:14.782 --> 00:13:16.330
You remember Christina Aguilera story?

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:19.420
I'm like, that was me,
can I see you on Friday?

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.960
Friday comes, we go and
we have the meeting,

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:24.360
and it lasted about 12 minutes,

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:27.520
and the CEO, Rich Santulli, said

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:29.350
literally threw us out,
this is a direct quote,

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:30.567
and it really pissed me off, he said,

00:13:30.567 --> 00:13:32.520
"If you think I'm giving two guys

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.810
who probably didn't break
1,000 on their SAT,"

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.250
which really pissed me
off, I got a 980 on my SAT,

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:38.757
so it really bothered me, he was like,

00:13:38.757 --> 00:13:41.030
"That's never happening," he threw us out.

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:42.280
And then the president, Jim,

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:44.297
who I got his daughter hooked
up, grabbed me and said,

00:13:44.297 --> 00:13:45.320
"That was unbelievable."

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.537
So we got thrown out
in 12 minutes, he goes,

00:13:47.537 --> 00:13:49.330
"Santulli doesn't give anyone 12 minutes.

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:50.970
I believe in this, but bring it to life.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.420
Come back next week."

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.090
So we came back next week,

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:56.340
and we realized, we could
never sell this idea

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.010
in a Powerpoint, 'cause this
guy sees 100 Powerpoints,

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:01.480
and entrepreneurs have to differentiate.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.310
So we brought in our own focus group,

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:05.370
and we set up a table
by the board room table,

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:06.950
and one by one, we had eight people come,

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:09.860
Carl Banks from The New York
Giants, Run from Run-DMC,

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.730
a powerful female real
estate mogul in New York,

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:14.300
and one by one, they
stood up and they said

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:16.880
they'd never buy a fraction
what NetJets was selling.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:20.441
But they'd buy a 25 hour jet
card, what we were offering.

00:14:20.441 --> 00:14:21.274
(snaps fingers)

00:14:21.274 --> 00:14:22.150
And he gave us a deal.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:25.393
And a year later, we did five
billion dollars in sales.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:27.980
- Then what happens?

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:32.573
- Then life changed.

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:35.510
The chips were purple,

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:37.890
and you know,

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.580
money's interesting, man.

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:41.910
It's interesting, we all
play for it, we all want it,

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:43.767
but we don't really even
know what we're playing for

00:14:43.767 --> 00:14:45.280
or why we want it.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.840
And then we get it, and it's confusing.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:49.800
And my relationship, we
think of relationships

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:51.720
in terms of people, casual relationship

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:52.980
with your mom or your dad,

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:56.210
we don't think of relationships
in terms of money and time.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.170
And I have a very good understanding

00:14:58.170 --> 00:14:59.590
of my relationship with time,

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:00.772
I know you just posted that,

00:15:00.772 --> 00:15:02.790
I've been talking about
that for a while, and money.

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:05.330
And all of a sudden, I
came into a lot of money.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:06.830
And I was single.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.350
And I had airplanes,
and I was in New York,

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:10.540
I could go on a date with this lady,

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:11.957
I'd be like, if it was
going good, I'd be like,

00:15:11.957 --> 00:15:13.180
"You want to go to Canada?"

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.580
(laughter)

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:15.413
What do you want to do?

00:15:15.413 --> 00:15:17.190
I mean, people are going to Studio 54,

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:18.720
trying to get to Studio 54, I'm like,

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.159
let's go to Hawaii tonight.

00:15:20.159 --> 00:15:22.700
(laughter)

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:24.142
So I had a great decade.

00:15:24.142 --> 00:15:26.392
(laughter)

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:28.753
- Fair enough!

00:15:29.884 --> 00:15:31.461
And then what happened?

00:15:31.461 --> 00:15:32.960
(laughter)

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:35.480
- And then I decided that

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:38.460
I didn't want to sell airplanes anymore.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:39.293
That wasn't,

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:42.290
I didn't feel like,

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.440
I felt like I wanted to
make a quarter by myself,

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:47.890
than have 75 cents in
the company I was in,

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:49.300
I want to go out on my own.

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:51.680
So I was training for a hundred mile run,

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:54.450
I realized, I had signed
up for the USA National

00:15:54.450 --> 00:15:55.970
Ultra Marathon Championship,

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:57.830
because you didn't have to qualify,

00:15:57.830 --> 00:15:59.700
you could just send $50 registration

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.020
and you're in the national
championship, and I'm like,

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:03.963
I'm competing in the
national championship?

00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:05.730
It's fuckin' unbelievable!

00:16:05.730 --> 00:16:08.330
Then I Googled how do you
run a hundred mile race,

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:09.860
and it came back with a year,

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.090
everything came back with a--

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:14.223
- How did that even hit
your radar to enter?

00:16:14.223 --> 00:16:15.056
Do you remember?

00:16:15.056 --> 00:16:16.020
- I went through a crazy
period of time, man.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.890
I was thinking about
giving all my money away,

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.360
I was thinking about charity,

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:20.740
I was just like,

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:21.573
I just, you know.

00:16:21.573 --> 00:16:22.559
- You were just trying to figure it out.

00:16:22.559 --> 00:16:23.392
- Trying to figure it out!

00:16:23.392 --> 00:16:24.225
And I was like,

00:16:24.225 --> 00:16:26.390
I want to do something big for charity.

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:28.810
And I didn't want to have a
bake sale or a golf outing,

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.210
my friends were all having foursomes,

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:32.950
I don't want to, I'm not a foursome guy.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:33.931
So--

00:16:33.931 --> 00:16:34.764
- The golf term, relax.

00:16:34.764 --> 00:16:35.597
- Yeah, I'm gonna--

00:16:35.597 --> 00:16:36.430
(laughter)

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:37.560
- Keep going.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:39.180
Just trying to help the kids at home.

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:40.013
Go ahead.

00:16:40.013 --> 00:16:42.170
- I wanted to run a hundred
miles without stopping.

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:43.119
- Yeah.

00:16:43.119 --> 00:16:44.210
- So I like, and what
would be the biggest race

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:46.890
to do that in, for me it was
the national championship.

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:48.940
So I Googled it, it gave me,

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:50.720
everything was like a
year training program,

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.430
the race was in 90 days.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.890
So I went from basically
couch to a hundred miles

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:56.290
in 90 days.

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:58.540
And during the training,
I did a lot of research

00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:00.440
around hydration and nutrition.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.560
And everything pointed at coconut water,

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:05.270
so I became the human guinea
pig for coconut water.

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:06.103
And like--

00:17:06.103 --> 00:17:06.936
- What year was this?

00:17:06.936 --> 00:17:10.440
- This was in 2006, 2006.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:11.900
Because when you run a hundred miles,

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:13.390
how many calories do I
have to take in an hour?

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:16.140
How much sodium, how many
fluid ounces do I need?

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.320
Coconut water was the solution, so anyway,

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:20.920
I finished the race,
when I got to mile 83,

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:23.320
I had seven toenails
floating around in my shoe.

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:26.840
But I don't negotiate my goals.

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:29.430
We don't negotiate our goals in this room.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:30.900
Whatever our goal is,

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.210
it's unwavering and the plot might change,

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:36.160
and the script might change,
but the goal doesn't change.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:39.200
So my goal wasn't 87 miles, or 83 miles,

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.780
so I finished it, and then I was like,

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:43.920
this coconut water shit, man,
there's something to this!

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.630
By the way, I was in a
wheelchair for four days

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:47.770
after the race, but I did finish it.

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:49.200
And I spent a year trying
to figure out how to--

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:50.033
- Do you have photos of
you in that wheelchair?

00:17:50.033 --> 00:17:50.910
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:51.840
- You ever post it?

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:52.949
- Yeah.

00:17:52.949 --> 00:17:53.782
- That's a good one.

00:17:53.782 --> 00:17:54.615
- Yeah, I had a whole,
did a documentary on it.

00:17:54.615 --> 00:17:55.448
- Go ahead.

00:17:55.448 --> 00:17:56.281
- You know.
- Yep.

00:17:56.281 --> 00:17:57.500
- Anyway, long story short,

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:58.800
I spent a year trying to figure out

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.150
how to import coconut water,

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:01.910
and I did get a 980 on my SAT,

